Adapted files (each carries a header naming its source file, the upstream
commit and what was changed):

  apps/host/src/engine-env.ts
      from packages/ai-provider/src/subscription/env.ts @ 9052297
  apps/host/src/engine-login.ts
      from packages/ai-provider/src/subscription/status.ts and contract.ts @ 9052297
  apps/web/src/mention.ts
      from packages/ui/src/doc-mention-model.ts @ 9052297

  third_party/ieumoffice/user-skills/, zip-gate/, agent-core/user-skill-names.ts
      Node-only skill import/export/template core, metered ZIP inflation and
      reserved names. Exact source commit and SHA-256 values are recorded in
      user-skills.manifest.json. Changes are explicit .ts relative imports,
      local package import paths and source attribution headers. Regenerate
      with scripts/vendor-user-skills.mjs; Workbench policy adapters are kept
      separately in apps/host/src.

  third_party/ieumoffice/hwp/, file-parse/hwp.ts
      HWP/HWPX save preservation and text/memo reading from pinned Ieum source.
      hwp.manifest.json records source and output hashes; regenerate with
      scripts/vendor-hwp-core.mjs. Workbench validation and revision policy
      remains separately in apps/host/src.

  third_party/ieumoffice/text-assets/
      Pure Node owned-image writer/atomic file helpers and HTML preview/MIME
      helpers. text-assets.manifest.json records source and output hashes;
      regenerate with scripts/vendor-text-assets.mjs. Workbench uses creation
      and reading, not destructive cleanup, to retain assets for document history.

The office editor pages (apps/office) are built from IeumOffice's source at
the commit named in vendor/ieumoffice.PINNED (fetched by scripts/fetch-ieum.sh,
not stored in this repository). Shared AI service ports are being integrated
separately; the editor screens retain their upstream source. The
Word editor gets a bridge to the Workbench gateway in place of its Electron
preload; the slides editor's screen and preload run in the page and its
editing code, written for Electron's main process, runs in the page's worker
with Workbench's platform modules (apps/office/src/platform) wherever it asks
Electron or Node for something. apps/office/src/slides/fonts.ts stands in for
apps/slides/src/main/fonts.ts and takes its Carlito line-metric rule and
script fallback from it. The spreadsheet editor runs the same way (screen and
preload in the page, editing code in the page's worker); its Rust xlsx engine
(apps/sheets/native/xlsx-engine), which IeumOffice runs as a separate program,
is compiled to WebAssembly from a copy with apps/office/native/xlsx-wasm/
wasi.patch applied (a temporary folder and sheet indexing without threads),
and apps/office/native/xlsx-wasm/src/lib.rs adapts the engine's request
dispatch from its src/main.rs. The built pages carry THIRD-PARTY-NOTICES.txt
with this NOTICE, the LICENSE, the font licences, the licences of the npm
packages bundled into them and of the Rust crates compiled into the engine.
The Hangul screen hosts the pinned offline rhwp-studio with Ieum's plugins.
Workbench supplies file/save/recovery channels without Electron. The host
and screen share manifest-verified rhwp WASM. The studio includes its rhwp
licence and third-party notices; scripts/build-hwp-studio.mjs verifies the
source pin and WASM hash and packages those files together.

The bundled `workbench-hwp` tool (apps/host/src/hwp-cli.ts) reuses the pinned
Ieum CLI's HWP operation catalog and headless editing session, including the
HWP renderer's skill, studio agent plugin and preservation modules. The
adapter lives in hwp-ops.mjs; scripts/build-host.mjs verifies the Ieum source
pin. It shares the same rhwp WASM with the internal editor.

The `ieumoffice` command line tool that Workbench can call is not part of
this repository; it ships inside an installed IeumOffice application.
